    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d508">

                <pb facs="0744" n="728" xml:id="pg728"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.4054/5–2147: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Durbrow</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-05-21T11:00:00+03:00">May 21, 1947—11 a.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1843. Applicant for immigration visa informed Embassy recently that Assistant
                    Chief of OVIR (exit visa issuing office) told
                    her that cases of “private citizens desiring to depart from Soviet Union are not
                    being considered as present time.” This additional evidence that wives of
                    American citizens (Embtel 1203 April 5) will
                    not be allowed to depart. Also indicates high level decision on whole question
                    of exit visas has been or is in process of being made.<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d508-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Durbrow</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d508-f1" n="1" xml:id="d508-f1-content">
                <p>The Chargé further reported in telegram 2058 from Moscow on June 7, noon, not
                    printed, that Soviet authorities were even preventing the departure of Americans
                    whose citizenship had been verified and who were mot dual nationals. Notes had
                    been written on 8 cases, involving 10 individuals, but the Chargé believed that,
                    because of the seriousness of the arbitrary action, Ambassador Smith should take
                    up the matter at a high level upon his return to the Embassy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
